@title What Eighteen Months of Four-Day Weeks Did to Our Support Team
@subtitle Halden Cartography blog
@author Priscilla Adeyemi-Koh, Head of Customer Support

When I first suggested a four-day week for the support team, I expected two kinds of resistance. Customers would worry that we were slowing down, and some managers would assume that people working fewer hours must care less. I was wrong about the second part, and I am still not sure I was right to be so confident about the first. This is what happened over eighteen months, including the parts I would rather not have had.

## Why we tried it

Our support team has 23 people, and the work is demanding in a way that is easy to underestimate. Every day we answer questions from customers who are stuck, frustrated or both. In 2024, annual attrition on the team was 21 percent. When we asked people why they were leaving, burnout was the reason we heard most often. Nobody left because they disliked the product or the customers. They left because the job had slowly taken over their evenings and weekends, and they could not see how it would stop.

We had already tried the usual fixes. We added training, rewrote the rota twice and introduced a wellness stipend that few people had time to use. None of them changed the basic shape of the week. When a colleague suggested we test a four-day week, I was skeptical, but I was also tired of watching good people leave for reasons we had not fixed. So I said yes to a pilot.

## How the schedule works

Everyone works Monday to Thursday, nine hours a day. That is 36 hours a week at full pay. We did not cut anyone's pay, and we did not ask people to squeeze their old hours into four days without a plan.

Friday is the hard part. Customers still write on Fridays, and some still expect a person to answer. We cover it with a rotating on-call group of four people. Those four work Friday, and each of them takes the following Monday off instead. Because the group rotates, the Friday coverage is spread across the team rather than landing on the same people every week.

We wrote the coverage rules down before the first week. The document ran to several pages and covered handoffs, escalation, public holidays and what happens when someone is sick on a Friday. It was dull to write, and it saved us many arguments later.

## What happened to the numbers

The pilot began on April 7, 2025, and ended on October 2, 2026, with the decision to make the schedule permanent. Median first response time fell from 3.4 hours to 2.9 hours. Customer satisfaction rose from 4.3 to 4.5 out of 5. Annual attrition fell from 21 percent to 9 percent, which on a team of 23 means we kept several more people than we otherwise would have. Sick days fell by 31 percent.

I want to be careful with these figures. A pilot is not a controlled experiment, and some of the gain may come simply from having a new arrangement that people cared about. But the direction was consistent across every measure we tracked, and that is the part I did not expect. I had assumed that fewer hours would mean slower answers. It did not.

## What got harder

It was not all good news. Thursday evenings became the weak point, because the handoffs to Friday's on-call group depended on notes people wrote at the end of a long day. Some of those notes were thin, and the on-call people spent their first hour reconstructing context. We fixed it by moving the handoff earlier in the day and turning the notes into a short template.

Two enterprise customers wanted guaranteed phone support on Fridays, and the schedule could not give them that without changing it. We hired two more people to cover it. That was the right call for those customers, but it also meant the four-day week had a real cost, and I think we should say so plainly rather than treat it as a footnote.

A few people also found nine-hour days tiring in the winter, when the light was gone before they finished. A nine-hour day in the dark is a long day. We are looking at whether a shorter Thursday in the darkest months would help, and we are asking people instead of guessing.

## If you try it

We are keeping the schedule, so this advice comes from experience rather than hope. Three things matter most.

1. Pilot for at least six months. Our first three months looked promising, and the winter problems only showed up later. A short trial hides the things that matter most.
2. Write the coverage rules before the first week. Almost every gap we found in the first month was something somebody had assumed another person would cover.
3. Measure customer metrics alongside team morale. If you track only how people feel, you can be fooled into thinking a schedule works. If you track only response times, you can miss the people who are quietly leaving. You need both.
